{"classes":["","diverted","regulated","reservoir"],"exclude":["diverted","reservoir"],"notes":{"diverted":"Hydro-diverted: most of the natural flow is taken out upstream, so the natural flood statistic overstates the flow.","regulated":"Flow controlled by a hydro scheme (storage dam, control gates or tailrace): natural flood statistics do not describe it.","reservoir":"Hydro lake or dam structure, not a river reach."},"schemes":[{"id":"kaimai","name":"Kaimai hydro scheme","operator":"Manawa Energy (formerly Trustpower)","note":"Run of river with daily storage. Western diversions: the Opuiaki Weir sends the Opuiaki River through Tunnel 4 (which also takes the Tauwharawhara Stream) into the Ngatuhoa Canal, fed too by the Ngatuhoa Weir; Tunnel 5 takes the canal to the upper Mangaonui Stream and Lake Mangaonui. Eastern diversions: the Omanawa weirs (Tunnel 2) and the Mangapapa weir on the upper Wairoa (REC2 name; Tunnel 3). The Lloyd Mandeno Canal takes Lake Mangaonui (up to 14.2 m3/s) to Lloyd Mandeno Power Station on the upper Wairoa; Lake Matariki and Lower Mangapapa lead into Lake McLaren, which also gets the Mangakarengorengo River through two tunnels (up to 7 m3/s). The Ruahihi Canal (up to 31.2 m3/s) takes Lake McLaren to Ruahihi Power Station. The Wairoa below the McLaren Falls dam had no required residual flow (150 l/s under the 2026 consents) and the Mangakarengorengo joins it just below the dam: it runs on release days (about 26 a year, the dam-release run) and when the weirs spill. EA Report 4 (2.8, pp. 10-11, Figure 2-9) confirms the stations and Lake McLaren: four stations (Lloyd Mandeno 1972, Lower Mangapapa 1979, Ruahihi 1981, Kaimai 5 1994), Lake McLaren held by a 26 m concrete arch dam across the lower Mangapapa River, a gated inlet into the 2.5 km canal to Ruahihi, and a bypass at the dam for \"recreational flows into the Wairoa River on set days each year\"; it names none of the intakes and gives no intake rate, residual flow, level or number of release days. Not modelled: the Tauwharawhara take (up to 283 l/s, a stream of about 2 km2), Lake Matariki, the short Lower Mangapapa bypass and Kaimai 5 (Report 4 does not say where it is). Intakes, flows and levels from the 2025-26 fast-track re-consenting documents (rf-6u2), read through web-search excerpts only and unconfirmed (fasttrack.govt.nz refuses scripts).","src":["https://www.manawaenergy.co.nz/kaimai-power-scheme","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaimai_hydro_power_scheme","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/1_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes/HMD%20Report%204%20-%20Hydro-power%20Schemes%20Background%20and%20Descriptions.pdf","https://www.fasttrack.govt.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0022/26653/Kaimai-HEPS-Appendix-A-Consent-Conditions-Final.pdf","https://www.fasttrack.govt.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0020/12863/B.05-Appendix-F-Existing-Resource-Consents.pdf.pdf","https://www.fasttrack.govt.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0022/12865/B.07-Appendix-H-Hydrology-Assessment-Tonkin-and-Taylor.pdf.pdf","https://www.fasttrack.govt.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0025/18745/Kaimai-Panel-Presentation.pdf"]}],"r":[[4,2,0,78],[59,2,0,78],[60,2,0,78],[61,2,0,78],[62,2,0,78],[63,2,0,78],[64,2,0,78],[97,2,0,80],[98,2,0,80],[99,2,0,80],[100,2,0,80],[101,2,0,80],[102,2,0,80],[103,2,0,80],[104,2,0,80],[243,2,0,95],[244,2,0,95],[245,2,0,95],[246,2,0,95],[247,2,0,95],[585,2,0,98],[586,2,0,98],[587,2,0,98],[588,2,0,98],[589,2,0,98],[590,2,0,98],[591,2,0,98],[592,2,0,98],[593,2,0,98],[594,2,0,97],[595,2,0,97],[596,2,0,97],[597,2,0,97],[598,2,0,97],[632,2,0,98],[750,2,0,99],[751,2,0,99],[752,2,0,99],[753,2,0,99],[754,2,0,99],[755,2,0,99],[826,2,0,99],[827,2,0,99],[853,2,0,99],[864,2,0,100],[1062,2,0,100],[1063,2,0,100],[1064,2,0,100],[1065,2,0,100],[1066,2,0,100],[1067,2,0,100],[1176,2,0,100],[1177,2,0,100],[1178,2,0,100],[1179,2,0,100],[1185,2,0,100],[1278,2,0,100],[1279,2,0,100],[1280,2,0,100],[1281,2,0,100],[1387,2,0,100],[1388,2,0,100],[1389,2,0,